[...]
> So I'd like to see some justification for why the verdical/non-verdial
> distinction must be made with every reference to {le/lo toldi}.

You won't see it from me.  In my opinion, the veridicality question is
rather secondary.

{le toldi} is "each of the butterflies in question", normally I would
say "the butterfly in question", but it could be more than one.  If it
happens not to be really a butterfly, it doesn't matter that much,
because the specificity should make clear what is the referent.

On the other hand {lo toldi} is "at least one of the things that are
butterflies", with no way of knowing which one.  Then it makes sense to
expect that you are talking about real butterflies, since that is the
only restriction you are imposing.

But the important difference is that one refers to one ore many specific
butterflies, and the other just to "some butterfly".

